From 5eacddb304ff6281786af6a1501d1b08e79339aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: v <v@v>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 11:50:42 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#143): weave everything the lower tile still owns

Patch 0113 (browser#131) made overlapping tiles EYE-CONSISTENT, which was the
real defect, but it paid too much for it. A submitted rect is not a window onto
the weave input; it is a SIDE-BY-SIDE PAIR whose eye split is the rect's own
horizontal midpoint, so 0113 could only trim ROWS -- and the survivor had to be
ONE full-width row band. Beside a corner occluder that flattens pixels nothing
covers, and a FULL-HEIGHT occluder flattens the tile outright (top_h == bot_h
== 0, keep empty, everything mono). David's verdict on the panel was "it looks
broken", and it was.

WHY THERE IS NO PROTOCOL CHANGE HERE. A per-entry SOURCE rect was investigated
and rejected. For a destination sub-rect D the two eye sources sit in the
scratch separated by half the pair width, so demanding one source rect whose own
midpoint splits them collapses to D.width == tileWidth -- exactly the full-width
case that already worked. And the deeper reason is not expressibility at all:
the shared window-sized weave input is already TEXEL-OVERWRITTEN where two tiles
overlap, so the lower tile's pixels are gone before the runtime ever reads them.
No wire format recovers them. Only RE-STAGING does. (A runtime branch holds the
investigated extension; it buys efficiency, not capability.)

And once you re-stage, the protocol is already enough: a repacked pair is
exactly as wide as its own destination sub-rect, so it lands at that sub-rect's
own window position and srcRect == dstRect.

1. GUILLOTINE. |topmost_pieces| is the row-band-major decomposition of the lower
   tile minus the occluder -- TOP / LEFT / RIGHT / BOTTOM, at most 4, and 4 is
   minimal for the interior case. Per single occluder: fully covered 0, edge
   full-width 1, full-height touching an edge 1 (0113: ZERO), full-width interior
   in y 2, full-height interior in x 2 (0113: ZERO), CORNER 2 (the reported case
   12), edge-not-full-width 3, interior frame 4. Several occluders iterate the
   same cut, capped at 8 rects per tile.

2. REPACK. For D = (dx,dy,dw,dh) of the tile (X,Y,W,H), a = dx-X, half = W/2:

     srcLeft  = (a/2,       dy-Y, dw/2, dh)  ->  dstLeft  = (dx,       dy, dw/2, dh)
     srcRight = (half+a/2,  dy-Y, dw/2, dh)  ->  dstRight = (dx+dw/2,  dy, dw/2, dh)

   Two 1:1 box copies out of the tile's OWN scratch (never out of the shared
   input, which is where the collision lives), one batch entry whose rect is D.
   The repacked pair is dw wide, so it fits inside D; D is disjoint from every
   upper tile's footprint by construction; the shared input becomes a globally
   disjoint mosaic. The runtime then splits D at dx + dw/2, which is precisely
   the boundary the repack wrote.

   THE FULL-WIDTH CASE IS THE IDENTITY, written out explicitly rather than left
   to fall out of the two-half form: one contiguous copy, bit-for-bit the call
   0113 made, including its pre-existing odd-W behaviour. So the NO-OVERLAP PATH
   IS BYTE-IDENTICAL -- 0113's own standard, and it stays 0114's.

3. EVERY CUT LANDS ON AN EVEN COLUMN. Load-bearing, and it comes from the
   runtime side rather than from taste: the three platform arms round an odd
   pair's halves DIFFERENTLY -- D3D11 splits rw/2.0f as a float (half-texel
   bilinear seam) while macOS and Android take an integer rw/2 and give the
   right eye one texel more. So an odd cut weaves differently per platform,
   which is the eye-asymmetry class #131/#143 exist to kill. Concretely: an odd
   OFFSET shifts one eye 1 source px = 2 destination px over the whole sub-rect
   (a false depth step); an odd WIDTH makes the runtime's own w/2 split disagree
   with what was written by a column (a 1-px stripe of the wrong eye at the
   seam). The occluder is snapped OUTWARD -- growing it only gives up pixels
   already doomed, shrinking it would under-cover and leave the #131 replica.
   An odd-width tile's residual last column is given to the withheld set, so it
   goes mono rather than odd; a full-width piece keeps it, since it takes the
   identity copy.

4. BUDGET. XR_WEAVE_SUBMIT_MAX_RECTS_DXR is 32 per call and the client chunks
   above it -- not a correctness problem, but every submit runs the runtime's
   process_atlas once, so N chunks is N whole-window weaves in one frame and a
   vendor predictor fed N partial pictures. One frame, one submit: 8 rects per
   tile, 32 per frame, largest-first demotion back to 0113's row band. Never
   silent -- a throttled line names the cap, the budget and the (unreachable)
   even-rule refusal separately, because the three want different fixes.

5. WHAT 0113 KEEPS. The z plumbing, own[] semantics, the total order, the
   fully-covered exits, and the keep-previous decline are untouched. The ROW
   BAND survives as the single demotion target. 0112's mono primitive is whole
   and still carries never-composed tiles, #120 mono-under-frost, the fully
   covered tile, every demotion, and the odd residual column -- its withheld[2]
   row-band array generalises to a LIST the decomposition accumulates as it
   removes, so it is the exact complement of what was submitted and needs no
   region difference to recover. The wish improves for free: the remainder is
   now inside union(rects) so the lens stays ON over it; no SetBatchWish change.
   BatchKeepPrevious is now declined STRUCTURALLY -- a repack erases the
   target's batch_last_copied_ record, because after a repack the input content
   at an address is a function of the whole decomposition.

6. TWO ARMS DO NOT DECOMPOSE, gated so it cannot happen silently. ANDROID
   stages by Skia draw at the full window rect and would corrupt its eyes
   without its own per-piece repack (which lands with the rest of that arm,
   blocked on #122 / tracked in #100). NON-BATCH providers have no shared input
   for a repack to address. Both stay on 0113's row band.

Also closes browser#144: 0113 left |weave_src_y| defined and unread on Android
(every consumer is inside a !IS_ANDROID arm), failing -Werror,-Wunused-variable
and turning main Android-red. Marked [[maybe_unused]] here, as is
|single_full_width| for the same reason; the desktop arms are byte-identical.

Verified: clang-cl -fsyntax-only on every touched C++ TU with the real ninja
flags -- the viz hook and the weave provider are error- and warning-CLEAN, and
the Windows GPU provider's error set is IDENTICAL to its unpatched baseline
(pre-existing generated-mojom drift only, none new). The three geometry helpers
were compiled verbatim into a standalone harness and run over 18 configurations
(every row of the table, odd tiles, odd occluder edges, odd origins, two
occluders): pieces disjoint and inside the tile, pieces disjoint from the
snapped occluder, pieces + withheld == the whole tile exactly, every copy 1:1
with its source inside the scratch and its destination inside its own piece,
the right eye's source never past the pair, the two halves tiling each piece
exactly, and the full-width copy literally the identity. 0 failures.

